<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johor is a state at the southern tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, which became the seat of a Malay sultanate after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>. Like Malacca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, it functioned as a commercial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a>. The site cites it as part of the trading tradition established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johor.jpg" alt="Johor" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mrmw, Mike Rohsopht · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Johor.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Johor covers nearly 19,166 square kilometres in the south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> to the east, the Straits of Johor to the south and the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> to the west. Tanjung Piai is the southernmost point of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>. The landscape is flat, broken by inselbergs and massifs, with Mount Ledang the highest point, and its tropical rainforests include the Endau-Rompin National Park. The area was known to people in Siam as Gangganu or Ganggayu, meaning Treasury of Gems, and Arab traders called it jauhar, from which the name Johor came. The Old Javanese Nagarakretagama called it Ujong Medini, land's end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A bronze bell estimated to date from about 150 CE was found near the Muar River, and the lost city of Kota Gelanggi, known from an old Malay manuscript, is placed by some in the forests of Kota Tinggi District. After the Portuguese took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511, Ala'udin Ri'ayat Shah II, a son of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-of-malacca">Mahmud Shah of Malacca</a>, founded the Johor Sultanate in 1528 by moving the court to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-river">Johor River</a>. It spanned the southern peninsula, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau-archipelago">Riau Archipelago</a> including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, and fought a three-way contest with the Portuguese and the Aceh Sultanate.</p><p>After Mahmud Shah III died, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> recognised one son as heir in 1818 and the British recognised the other the following year. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> established Johor's modern borders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor">Sultan Abu Bakar</a> wrote its state constitution in 1895, and in 1914 it became a British protected state within the Unfederated Malay States. Japanese forces invaded in January 1942 after the Battle of Muar. Johor joined the Malayan Union in 1946 and the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> in 1948, and became part of independent Malaya in 1957 and of Malaysia in 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Johor is a state of Malaysia and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> headed by a sultan, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-bahru">Johor Bahru</a> as the state capital and Muar as the royal capital. It is divided into ten districts. The 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 4,100,900 people, making it the second most populous state. Malay is the official language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the state religion, and the population includes Malay, Chinese and Indian communities. Johor keeps a private army, the Royal Johor Military Force, which has protected its rulers since 1886. In 2008 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> awarded Pedra Branca to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aceh attacked Portuguese Malacca repeatedly between 1537 and 1629 without success, and fought a long three-way struggle with <b>Johor</b> for control of the strait.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Johor</b>'s constitution of 1895, issued under Sultan Abu Bakar, forbade any ruler from surrendering the state or any part of it to a foreign power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Muzaffar Shah was invited to rule the north of the peninsula as Sultan of Perak, and Alauddin Riayat Shah II founded the Sultanate of <b>Johor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The commercial infrastructure Srivijaya developed, with dependable harbour facilities, predictable customs procedures and the protection of merchant shipping, set the pattern for the entrepôt system later followed by Malacca, <b>Johor</b>, Penang and finally Singapore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultanate itself is traditionally credited to Sharif ul-Hashim, also called Sayyid Abu Bakr, an Arab scholar said in the Sulu genealogies to have come by way of <b>Johor</b>, who married Baguinda's daughter and took the title of sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Classical Malay, also called Court Malay, was the literary standard of Malacca and then of <b>Johor</b>, which is why it is sometimes called Malacca, Johor or Riau Malay.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
